As a student who is passionate about learning new things, connecting with people, and improving my skills through coding events and hackathons, my journey with the SIH Internal Hackathon was bit inspiring and lot disappointing.

One day, a group of seniors visited our class and announced the SIH Internal Hackathon. After hearing about it, I was extremely excited to participate. However, as a 1st-year student, I don’t knew anyone in my class, so i thought forming a team is impossible.

Later, when the registration period ended, the deadline was extended specifically for 1st-year students. I felt as if I had been given a second chance to prove something and I was genuinely interested in.

With gaining of confidence, I approached two classmates, A and B. They replied we also equally interested in hackathons, although they were not familiar with these. Since the team formation rules required at least two female members, So I contacted several people and eventually found two classmates, C and D, who agreed to join our team.

College provided a dedicated laboratory for hackathon teams, which was a great initiative and gave us a productive environment to work in.

From that point onward, I dedicated myself completely to the project. I spent two sleepless nights researching, discussing ideas with my teammates, and preparing our solution. At the age of 16, I can proudly say that I created a professional presentation containing hyperlinks, flowcharts, and even a working demo website.

I openly acknowledge that I used AI assistance for certain backend functionalities in the demo website. However, I have bit knowledge about frameworks and technologies, I also knew how the system is designed,and how it can be scaled into a real-world application in the future.

Our project was submitted under the category "Student Innovation in the Service Sector" with the theme "Miscellaneous." The idea focused on an AI-powered smart healthcare coordination platform designed to connect patients, hospitals, doctors, and emergency services through an integrated ecosystem with lot of microservices.

I was so confident about our idea, technical approach, presentation, and prototype of demo website.

A day before the presentation, a
message was posted in the college hackathon WhatsApp group stating that each team would receive only 6 minutes for their presentation. This made me nervous because our project involved multiple interconnected services.

Even though when our presentation began, everything went exceptionally well. I confidently explained the concept, the technical approach and product architecture, After that.The jury members asked several questions, and I answered each one with detailed explanations. Although the allocated time was 6 minutes, our discussion continued for nearly 12–15 minutes because the depth of the project and work i did and knowledge that i had on my project.

The jury members discussed about our project  themselves and even told us:

"After moving to the next step, continue with this presentation and add if any changes required. It is very good,Try to convert this demo into a real-world application."

Hearing those words gave us tremendous confidence.

However, later that afternoon, when the list of 50 shortlisted teams was announced, our team was not included.

I was deeply disappointed.

One of the selected projects was "x" by Team "A...D...V........" from 1st year, which focused on helping farmers just to find fair prices for selling vegetables and fruits in the market. While I respect their effort and idea, I was surprised because I genuinely believed many other projects, including ours, demonstrated stronger innovation, technical depth, and implementation.

Out of curiosity, I requested a copy of their presentation from one of the team members. After reviewing it, I was even more surprised and wondered what specific factors had made it stand out among so many strong submissions.

The following day, I spoke with another member from shortlisted team and casually asked about their experience. His response surprised me. He said that their presentation was fairly normal, they themselves were not expecting selection, and during the Q&A session, whenever they did not know an answer, they simply responded:

"We will think about it in the future and implement it."

The jury members replied , okay and wished atb , and concluded the presentation within 5–6 minutes without further discussion.

After hearing this, I became even more confused.

My intention is not to criticize or disrespect any selected team. Every team deserves appreciation for their efforts. However, as someone who invested significant time, energy, and passion into building a detailed solution, I genuinely want to understand the evaluation process.

Therefore, my question to the jury members is:

After reviewing so many capable and innovative projects, what specific factors influenced the selection decision ? What made only that project stand out for an big event like Smart India Hackathon (SIH) 2026 from 1st-year ?
